1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
It's the military's high-tech vanishing act.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,000
A masterful blend of contours and composites

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
that makes aircraft invisible to radar.

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:15,000
This sky-high sleight of man is now coming down to Earth

5
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:18,000
and setting out on the high seas.

6
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Daunting. Deadly. Top secret.

7
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Now, Stealth Technology on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Sleek, lethal, powerful, the F-822 Raptor opens a new era in stealth technology, the revolutionary

9
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:51,080
concept that's changed the face of aerial warfare since the 1990s.

10
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:55,380
Compared to anything else that's out there, this airplane is head and shoulders above

11
00:00:55,381 --> 00:00:56,081
those jets.

12
00:00:56,082 --> 00:00:59,620
We can get to places very quickly before people would expect us,

13
00:01:00,060 --> 00:01:02,360
and with stealth there, they don't know that we're going to be there.

14
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:05,000
The Raptor, like all stealth aircraft,

15
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:08,220
reduces its observability in five distinct areas.

16
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:15,120
Visual, acoustic, heat, infrared, and most of all, radar.

17
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:20,220
Radar, short for radio detecting and ranging,

18
00:01:20,840 --> 00:01:24,380
locates an object by transmitting ultra-high frequency radio waves.

19
00:01:24,381 --> 00:01:28,940
By measuring the time taken for the radio waves to be reflected back to the transmitter,

20
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:32,820
the location, speed, and size of the object can be determined.

21
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:36,440
One of the best ways to reduce the chance of detection

22
00:01:36,441 --> 00:01:41,560
is to create aircraft with the smallest possible radar cross-section, or RCS.

23
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:45,920
An aircraft's RCS can be reduced in two fundamental ways.

24
00:01:46,460 --> 00:01:49,620
First, specially designed contours built into the airframe

25
00:01:49,621 --> 00:01:52,120
help deflect radar away from the source.

26
00:01:53,060 --> 00:01:56,060
The earliest stealth aircraft accomplished this using flat panels.

27
00:01:56,820 --> 00:01:59,140
The latest generation of stealth has curved panels.

28
00:01:59,980 --> 00:02:05,000
The second way for a plane to minimize its radar cross-section is through the materials that coat the airframe.

29
00:02:06,100 --> 00:02:10,160
Those materials help absorb or trap radar signals rather than reflect them.

30
00:02:11,080 --> 00:02:15,760
The composition of these radar-absorbing materials, or RAM, remains classified.

31
00:02:15,761 --> 00:02:24,840
with the fa-22 raptor stealth technology has come of age manufactured by lockheed martin in

32
00:02:24,841 --> 00:02:31,480
partnership with boeing and pratt and whitney it measures more than 62 feet long with a wingspan of

33
00:02:31,481 --> 00:02:38,760
more than 44 feet yet its radar signature is smaller than a sparrows in the past achieving

34
00:02:38,761 --> 00:02:44,540
such invisibility required compromising aerodynamic performance case in point the

35
00:02:44,541 --> 00:02:48,140
Raptor's revolutionary predecessor, the F-117A.

36
00:02:48,740 --> 00:02:52,640
But the F-22 is as speedy and nimble as it is stealthy.

37
00:02:53,060 --> 00:02:58,660
If you compare the F-22 to a F-117, you'll notice that ours is very smooth, curved shapes,

38
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:02,600
and basically where that helps us is in the aerodynamic capabilities of the airplane.

39
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:08,780
We are much more agile, much more capable than the F-117, which is basically a series

40
00:03:08,781 --> 00:03:13,560
of flat plates, which greatly affects their aerodynamic agility capability.

41
00:03:13,561 --> 00:03:17,560
Designing a stealth aircraft is really not rocket science.

42
00:03:17,561 --> 00:03:19,560
The first thing we do is we take care of shaping.

43
00:03:19,561 --> 00:03:21,560
Once you take care of the shaping portion of it,

44
00:03:21,561 --> 00:03:23,560
you've done about 90% of the stealth.

45
00:03:23,561 --> 00:03:25,560
And in the area of shaping, what we worry about

46
00:03:25,561 --> 00:03:27,560
is what we call planform shaping.

47
00:03:29,560 --> 00:03:32,560
Planform shaping is an airframe design strategy

48
00:03:32,561 --> 00:03:35,560
in which leading edges are aligned along identical angles.

49
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:38,560
The goal is to predict and control the direction

50
00:03:38,561 --> 00:03:40,560
that radar waves bounce off surfaces,

51
00:03:40,561 --> 00:03:45,060
surfaces, a phenomenon called scattering.

52
00:03:45,061 --> 00:03:48,160
The Raptor sports another key element of effective shaping.

53
00:03:48,161 --> 00:03:51,240
Unlike conventional planes, which feature protruding navigation and

54
00:03:51,241 --> 00:03:55,200
communications gear, the Raptor integrates such equipment seamlessly

55
00:03:55,201 --> 00:03:57,080
within its airframe.

56
00:03:57,081 --> 00:04:00,340
As you look at the airplane, you notice that it's basically smooth.

57
00:04:00,341 --> 00:04:03,680
There are no antennas or anything sticking out of the airplane that you

58
00:04:03,681 --> 00:04:05,420
would see on other aircraft.

59
00:04:05,421 --> 00:04:09,620
And that's because we've taken all the antennas and sensors on the

60
00:04:09,621 --> 00:04:13,760
airplane and they're embedded in the leading edges, trailing edges in the aircraft itself.

61
00:04:14,540 --> 00:04:18,973
You can see right here, this large panel is actually our UHF radio antenna.

62
00:04:18,974 --> 00:04:19,800
There's one on

63
00:04:19,801 --> 00:04:21,918
the bottom here, there's one on top of the wing.

64
00:04:21,919 --> 00:04:24,080
If you were to look at an F-15 or F-16, you'd see

65
00:04:24,081 --> 00:04:28,500
a blade antenna and that's what they're using, where ours are smooth like this.

66
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:35,120
Using the Raptor's advanced stealth technology, a pilot can soar undetected in enemy airspace

67
00:04:35,121 --> 00:04:37,060
long enough to deliver his deadly arsenal

68
00:04:37,061 --> 00:04:39,260
and return home unscathed.

69
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:41,140
The reason we carry all the weapons

70
00:04:41,141 --> 00:04:43,420
internal is for stealth reasons.

71
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:45,020
With everything

72
00:04:45,021 --> 00:04:47,080
internal of the airplane, you don't have an increased

73
00:04:47,081 --> 00:04:49,100
radar cross-section, so people can't see you

74
00:04:49,101 --> 00:04:50,960
as well. When it comes time to employ

75
00:04:50,961 --> 00:04:52,980
the weapon, the doors open

76
00:04:52,981 --> 00:04:55,120
quickly, the missile is released, and then the doors

77
00:04:55,121 --> 00:04:57,160
close back up to put us back in that stealthy

78
00:04:57,161 --> 00:04:59,120
configuration, so people can't

79
00:04:59,121 --> 00:04:59,821
see us.

80
00:05:01,020 --> 00:05:03,140
Since straight lines or seams on a

81
00:05:03,141 --> 00:05:04,700
plane easily reflect radar,

82
00:05:05,120 --> 00:05:07,780
Every door on the Raptor features sawtoothed edging,

83
00:05:08,440 --> 00:05:11,740
from weapons bays and landing gear to maintenance access panels.

84
00:05:13,960 --> 00:05:17,520
The Raptor's cockpit canopy is another triumph of stealth design.

85
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:20,440
Radar can penetrate a conventional canopy

86
00:05:20,441 --> 00:05:23,520
and reflect off the cockpit interior and the pilot himself.

87
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:27,620
To counter this effect, previous stealth generations

88
00:05:27,621 --> 00:05:29,640
were forced to compromise the pilot's view.

89
00:05:30,520 --> 00:05:33,760
But the Raptor's radically redesigned 11-foot-long canopy

90
00:05:33,761 --> 00:05:36,880
grants the pilot a 360-degree view.

91
00:05:40,880 --> 00:05:43,520
To make sure that we don't get scattering from the cockpit

92
00:05:43,521 --> 00:05:45,420
or from the pilot, the pilot's helmet,

93
00:05:45,860 --> 00:05:48,220
we put specialized coatings on the canopy

94
00:05:48,221 --> 00:05:50,360
that provide it the conductivity

95
00:05:50,361 --> 00:05:53,340
so it looks just like a metal plate

96
00:05:53,341 --> 00:05:55,880
and energy reflects off of that

97
00:05:55,881 --> 00:05:58,600
rather than going into the canopy and reflecting back out.

98
00:05:58,601 --> 00:06:07,500
The Raptor's stealth technology is coupled with an advanced propulsion system called Super Cruise.

99
00:06:07,900 --> 00:06:17,406
New Pratt & Whitney engines with increased thrust-to-weight ratios enable the Raptor to cruise for extended periods at supersonic speeds without the added thrust of

100
00:06:17,407 --> 00:06:18,160
afterburners.

101
00:06:19,240 --> 00:06:26,920
Earlier jet fighters, known as legacy aircraft, use afterburners when they need an extra boost for carrier liftoffs or for supersonic speed.

102
00:06:28,600 --> 00:06:36,720
Typically, when we go into an afterburner mode on a jet engine, that's accomplished

103
00:06:36,721 --> 00:06:43,440
by effectively just squirting jet fuel into the exhaust system and lighting it off.

104
00:06:43,441 --> 00:06:48,800
We don't need to do that to be able to go super cruise.

105
00:06:48,801 --> 00:06:55,800
Super cruise enables the Raptor to go faster, farther, and longer than any previous jet.

106
00:06:55,801 --> 00:07:04,480
The longest a legacy aircraft can continuously fire its afterburners is about 15 minutes.

107
00:07:06,060 --> 00:07:09,800
The Raptor can operate in supercruise mode for almost a half hour.

108
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:15,360
A lot more sustainment, a lot more speed on the battlefield over a longer period of time.

109
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:23,600
Between stealth and supercruise, we have the ability of surprise or the capability of surprise.

110
00:07:23,601 --> 00:07:26,840
is people don't know we're there and we can get there very quickly,

111
00:07:27,420 --> 00:07:30,040
and they just don't have the time to react to what we're doing.

112
00:07:31,740 --> 00:07:36,620
Pilots can get a taste of the F-A-22's combat prowess here at the Raptor's Nest.

113
00:07:37,440 --> 00:07:41,580
This cockpit demonstrator shows the advantages of stealth technology in combat.

114
00:07:42,940 --> 00:07:48,540
We're going to fly a practice mission here to give you an idea of what the F-A-22 is capable of doing.

115
00:07:49,220 --> 00:07:51,340
We're at 40,000 feet right now.

116
00:07:51,341 --> 00:07:55,040
The Raptor's exact radar signature is classified,

117
00:07:55,660 --> 00:07:58,820
so the display features a notional or approximate range.

118
00:07:59,560 --> 00:08:02,720
We've got the own ship, the F-822,

119
00:08:03,080 --> 00:08:05,000
right here in the middle of this defensive display

120
00:08:05,001 --> 00:08:09,380
with this blue notional signature ring around it.

121
00:08:10,380 --> 00:08:13,880
That indicates that the airplane is a stealthy airplane.

122
00:08:14,600 --> 00:08:17,420
Just a flip of a switch shows the difference stealth makes.

123
00:08:18,020 --> 00:08:19,500
With the Raptor's stealth disabled,

124
00:08:19,501 --> 00:08:22,320
its radar signature more than doubles in size.

125
00:08:24,120 --> 00:08:25,440
But in stealth mode,

126
00:08:25,880 --> 00:08:27,720
the Raptor can infiltrate enemy territory,

127
00:08:28,100 --> 00:08:31,080
destroy targets, and withdraw before it's detected.

128
00:08:32,920 --> 00:08:35,000
Even if confronted by an enemy fighter,

129
00:08:35,640 --> 00:08:38,160
the pilot can trust in the Raptor's agility and weapons

130
00:08:38,161 --> 00:08:41,800
to get the first look, the first shot, and the first kill.

131
00:08:43,980 --> 00:08:48,120
We can rule the day as an air-dominant fighter in this century.

132
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:52,940
This is a fighter pilot's dream.

133
00:08:55,180 --> 00:08:58,300
The dream of inventing the ultimate countermeasure to radar

134
00:08:58,301 --> 00:09:01,060
dates back to World War II and the Battle of Britain.

135
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:06,740
In 1940, the Nazi juggernaut was poised to control all of Europe.

136
00:09:07,680 --> 00:09:10,520
Only England and the Royal Air Force stood in Hitler's way.

137
00:09:11,480 --> 00:09:15,080
But the RAF was just one-tenth the size of the German air force,

138
00:09:15,460 --> 00:09:16,380
the mighty Luftwaffe.

139
00:09:19,500 --> 00:09:22,560
For nearly three months, the RAF and the Luftwaffe

140
00:09:22,561 --> 00:09:24,740
waged daily battles above Great Britain

141
00:09:24,741 --> 00:09:26,040
for control of the skies.

142
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:30,940
Although Germany had a tremendous numerical advantage

143
00:09:30,941 --> 00:09:34,240
in planes and pilots, British tactics and technology

144
00:09:34,241 --> 00:09:34,941
were superior.

145
00:09:37,540 --> 00:09:39,340
Hermann Goering, the head of the Luftwaffe,

146
00:09:39,740 --> 00:09:42,160
thought it was uncanny that the RAF always seemed

147
00:09:42,161 --> 00:09:45,080
to be in the ideal position to intercept German air attacks.

148
00:09:45,700 --> 00:09:46,680
It wasn't uncanny.

149
00:09:47,340 --> 00:09:48,040
It was radar.

150
00:09:49,500 --> 00:09:54,500
The RAF had the huge advantage of knowing where and when the Luftwaffe would attack.

151
00:09:54,501 --> 00:09:59,680
They could even determine the size, speed, and heading of the attack force and be ready

152
00:09:59,681 --> 00:10:04,340
to pounce on it from above.

153
00:10:04,341 --> 00:10:13,340
Without control of the skies, Adolf Hitler was forced to abort the invasion of England.

154
00:10:13,341 --> 00:10:17,520
Since the end of World War II, radar has made it nearly impossible for warplanes to attack

155
00:10:17,521 --> 00:10:20,080
an enemy without being detected well in advance.

156
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:25,700
In the late 50s, the U.S. learned of great advances

157
00:10:25,701 --> 00:10:27,820
in Soviet radar and missile technology.

158
00:10:29,900 --> 00:10:33,420
The wake-up call occurred in an era when U-2 spy planes

159
00:10:33,421 --> 00:10:35,020
were flying over the Soviet Union.

160
00:10:35,660 --> 00:10:38,600
The U-2 was one of the great advances in aircraft technology.

161
00:10:39,380 --> 00:10:43,700
It was an incredible leap in an ability to fly over an enemy's territory,

162
00:10:43,701 --> 00:10:46,580
fly above his ability to see you on radar,

163
00:10:46,581 --> 00:10:51,440
fly above his ability to employ fighters against you

164
00:10:51,441 --> 00:10:53,560
at a key moment in the Cold War.

165
00:10:54,040 --> 00:10:58,920
But on May 1, 1960, U-2 pilot Francis Gary Powers

166
00:10:58,921 --> 00:11:01,440
was shot down by a Soviet surface-to-air missile.

167
00:11:02,140 --> 00:11:03,940
It marked the first time in history

168
00:11:03,941 --> 00:11:05,740
that a missile had brought down a plane.

169
00:11:07,520 --> 00:11:08,700
Powers survived the crash

170
00:11:08,701 --> 00:11:11,080
and was convicted of espionage in Moscow.

171
00:11:13,040 --> 00:11:15,320
U-2 flights over Russia were halted.

172
00:11:15,321 --> 00:11:23,500
Two things, I think, really precipitated us to really go after stealth as a matter of national priority,

173
00:11:24,120 --> 00:11:31,900
and that was the downing of the U-2s and the realization that the penetrating bombers that we had

174
00:11:31,901 --> 00:11:35,380
could no longer penetrate the critical Soviet Union airspace.

175
00:11:37,540 --> 00:11:41,420
Soviet advances in radar and surface-to-air missile technology

176
00:11:41,421 --> 00:11:46,400
now made it harder for the U.S. Air Force's Strategic Air Command, or SAC,

177
00:11:46,700 --> 00:11:48,900
to accomplish one of its primary missions,

178
00:11:50,020 --> 00:11:51,600
penetrating Soviet airspace

179
00:11:51,601 --> 00:11:55,220
and reaching key military targets deep within the Soviet Union.

180
00:12:01,560 --> 00:12:04,500
Leaders in the Pentagon reasoned that if the Soviet Union

181
00:12:04,501 --> 00:12:08,620
no longer feared the U.S. Air Force's ability to retaliate after an attack,

182
00:12:09,180 --> 00:12:11,980
it might be tempted to launch a nuclear first strike.

183
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:17,220
It became a top U.S. priority to develop tactics or planes

184
00:12:17,221 --> 00:12:21,620
that would circumvent Soviet radar defenses and shift the balance of power.

185
00:12:22,960 --> 00:12:27,200
In the 1970s, you had the Air Force saying,

186
00:12:27,201 --> 00:12:32,600
we want a capability to be able to go into a hostile area without radar seeing us.

187
00:12:33,560 --> 00:12:38,320
In cooperation with the Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency, or DARPA,

188
00:12:38,620 --> 00:12:41,060
a scientific bureau within the Defense Department,

189
00:12:41,660 --> 00:12:44,260
the Air Force initiated a special research program.

190
00:12:45,300 --> 00:12:48,280
It asked the leading military aircraft manufacturers

191
00:12:48,281 --> 00:12:50,380
to present proposals for a warplane

192
00:12:50,381 --> 00:12:52,680
with the smallest possible radar signature.

193
00:12:54,540 --> 00:12:57,440
Ironically, one of the companies not asked to enter the competition

194
00:12:57,441 --> 00:13:01,320
was the most respected secret think tank in the aerospace industry,

195
00:13:02,280 --> 00:13:03,420
Lockheed Skunk Works.

196
00:13:04,400 --> 00:13:07,940
Skunk Works was named after a foul-smelling secret moonshine still

197
00:13:07,941 --> 00:13:10,540
featured in Al Capp's Lil Abner comic strip.

198
00:13:12,440 --> 00:13:15,120
It had earned a reputation as the preeminent creator

199
00:13:15,121 --> 00:13:17,100
of exotic high-performance aircraft,

200
00:13:17,560 --> 00:13:19,600
including jets like the high-flying U-2.

201
00:13:21,400 --> 00:13:23,160
We were not asked to participate.

202
00:13:23,520 --> 00:13:25,960
Basically, they went to the fighter houses of the day.

203
00:13:26,080 --> 00:13:27,580
People had fighters in production.

204
00:13:27,740 --> 00:13:28,440
We didn't.

205
00:13:28,540 --> 00:13:30,240
We were not currently building a fighter.

206
00:13:30,960 --> 00:13:33,160
So we were not in the ballgame, so to speak.

207
00:13:33,161 --> 00:13:38,020
But Skunk Works had a secret.

208
00:13:38,800 --> 00:13:44,940
It had already ventured into low observable technology for their number one client, the Central Intelligence Agency.

209
00:13:45,920 --> 00:13:51,700
Amazingly, throughout the 1950s, the CIA had its own air force of sophisticated spy planes.

210
00:13:52,600 --> 00:13:56,500
Their greatest spy plane was the SR-71 Blackbird.

211
00:13:56,501 --> 00:14:03,280
It was capable of speeds in excess of 2,000 miles per hour at altitudes over 80,000 feet.

212
00:14:04,080 --> 00:14:06,300
It was specially shaped to defeat radar.

213
00:14:07,100 --> 00:14:10,940
Its wings, tail, and fuselage were loaded with special composite materials

214
00:14:10,941 --> 00:14:14,000
and iron ferrites that absorbed radar energy.

215
00:14:15,020 --> 00:14:18,960
In addition, the leading edges of its wings were lined with a radar-absorbing structure,

216
00:14:19,360 --> 00:14:21,800
or RAS, that trapped radar energy.

217
00:14:21,801 --> 00:14:30,780
Skunk Works' problem was that all the Blackbird's design features were classified and property of the CIA.

218
00:14:31,780 --> 00:14:38,620
But Ben Rich, the head of Skunk Works, persisted and eventually won the CIA's permission to reveal the secret data.

219
00:14:39,600 --> 00:14:43,440
Lockheed was permitted to enter the stealth or low-observable competition.

220
00:14:43,980 --> 00:14:47,740
Now all Skunk Works had to do to win was build an invisible airplane.

221
00:14:48,720 --> 00:14:51,500
Fortunately, they had someone who thought he could do it.

222
00:14:51,800 --> 00:14:58,560
The march of stealth technology began with one revolutionary aircraft,

223
00:14:59,160 --> 00:15:02,040
born of a secrecy as black as its titanium airframe.

224
00:15:02,900 --> 00:15:05,920
What the right flyer was to aviation in its earliest days,

225
00:15:06,500 --> 00:15:09,740
the F-117 Nighthawk has been to stealth technology.

226
00:15:10,580 --> 00:15:13,940
From a historical standpoint, people are going to look back on the F-117,

227
00:15:14,360 --> 00:15:17,040
and it will certainly be a milestone.

228
00:15:17,041 --> 00:15:21,140
And it's just amazing to me that the thing was designed over 30 years ago,

229
00:15:21,141 --> 00:15:25,820
and it is still, bar none, the best out there at what it does.

230
00:15:26,200 --> 00:15:30,880
And you need somebody to go in and provide precision munitions.

231
00:15:31,220 --> 00:15:34,160
There's nobody else to call, in my opinion, other than the F-117.

232
00:15:34,860 --> 00:15:36,800
Developed by Lockheed Skunk Works,

233
00:15:37,240 --> 00:15:40,900
the F-117 was the first stealth aircraft to be tested in combat.

234
00:15:41,760 --> 00:15:43,640
On December 19, 1989,

235
00:15:44,300 --> 00:15:46,980
eight Nighthawks took part in Operation Just Cause,

236
00:15:47,400 --> 00:15:48,920
the military invasion of Panama.

237
00:15:51,140 --> 00:15:56,780
But it was Operation Desert Storm in 1991 that brought these fighters public acclaim.

238
00:15:57,480 --> 00:16:03,120
In nearly 1,300 missions, they accounted for 40% of all targets eliminated in the war.

239
00:16:05,200 --> 00:16:10,200
Despite the presence of hundreds of anti-aircraft artillery batteries and surface-to-air missiles,

240
00:16:10,720 --> 00:16:14,460
not a single stealth fighter was lost, proving the old axiom,

241
00:16:14,960 --> 00:16:16,580
you can't hit what you can't see.

242
00:16:21,140 --> 00:16:29,300
Lieutenant Colonel Joseph Salata was based with the 37th Tactical Fighter Wing Night Stalker Squadron in Saudi Arabia.

243
00:16:29,860 --> 00:16:34,180
He flew the F-117 in the first wave of black jets to attack Baghdad.

244
00:16:34,500 --> 00:16:39,060
We sort of believed in stealth technology, but we hadn't really proven it at the time.

245
00:16:39,180 --> 00:16:45,860
We had done it in radar tests and so forth, but never in actual combat when there were actual bullets flying at you.

246
00:16:46,120 --> 00:16:50,220
So there was probably just a little bit of nervousness there.

247
00:16:51,140 --> 00:16:54,040
Until we saw what happened on the first night,

248
00:16:54,580 --> 00:16:56,680
and we saw how successful we really could be,

249
00:16:56,681 --> 00:17:01,240
and saw that the Skunk Works really did design a great stealth airplane.

250
00:17:02,680 --> 00:17:07,600
The F-117s destroyed Saddam Hussein's key command and control centers,

251
00:17:08,040 --> 00:17:10,200
the heart of his integrated air defense system,

252
00:17:10,640 --> 00:17:12,200
and vital communication centers.

253
00:17:13,080 --> 00:17:18,500
In just one night, the black jets all but eliminated Iraq's ability to wage a coordinated war.

254
00:17:18,501 --> 00:17:23,400
We had mission success rates that were unprecedented.

255
00:17:24,180 --> 00:17:27,420
You could almost guarantee that a target was going to be taken out by a stealth

256
00:17:27,421 --> 00:17:32,820
as long as we didn't have problems with weather or maintenance on our aircraft or something like that.

257
00:17:33,400 --> 00:17:37,880
As long as we got to the target, we had a very high success rate of killing that target.

258
00:17:39,020 --> 00:17:42,320
The mysterious black jets sent shockwaves around the globe,

259
00:17:42,760 --> 00:17:44,920
putting military planners and governments on notice

260
00:17:44,921 --> 00:17:47,360
that every defensive radar system in the world

261
00:17:47,361 --> 00:17:49,520
was now useless against stealth technology.

262
00:17:50,200 --> 00:17:52,600
This really changes the nature of warfare.

263
00:17:52,960 --> 00:17:54,800
To be able to go into a foreign country,

264
00:17:55,480 --> 00:17:57,400
be able to destroy a target

265
00:17:57,401 --> 00:17:59,680
without them even knowing you're there

266
00:17:59,681 --> 00:18:00,720
until the target's destroyed.

267
00:18:01,120 --> 00:18:03,160
They didn't know we destroyed those targets.

268
00:18:03,280 --> 00:18:05,420
They didn't know these planes were in-country

269
00:18:05,421 --> 00:18:07,540
until there was smoke over Baghdad.

270
00:18:08,860 --> 00:18:11,660
Twelve years later, in Operation Iraqi Freedom,

271
00:18:11,661 --> 00:18:14,660
the F-117 once again ruled the skies.

272
00:18:14,661 --> 00:18:18,660
As far as what was it like to fly the stealth

273
00:18:18,661 --> 00:18:21,660
in Operation Iraqi Freedom, well, I'll be honest,

274
00:18:21,661 --> 00:18:24,660
I was a little bit nervous the first night.

275
00:18:24,661 --> 00:18:26,660
And so you rely on your training,

276
00:18:26,661 --> 00:18:28,660
and everything just kind of starts to flow,

277
00:18:28,661 --> 00:18:30,660
just like a normal type of sortie.

278
00:18:30,661 --> 00:18:32,660
Throughout that whole campaign,

279
00:18:32,661 --> 00:18:35,660
over 100 sorties and, you know,

280
00:18:35,661 --> 00:18:38,660
500 or 600 hours of flying,

281
00:18:38,661 --> 00:18:44,740
The aircraft never took a scratch, and we were able to have about a 92% hit rate.

282
00:18:44,741 --> 00:18:51,240
Every combat mission that I flew in OIF, I never felt like I was being tracked.

283
00:18:51,241 --> 00:18:55,840
And I think from that standpoint, LO paid for itself right there.

284
00:18:55,841 --> 00:18:59,420
You can get in there and get out and get your job done.

285
00:18:59,421 --> 00:19:06,520
The combat success of the F-117 has set the standard for all low observable technology.

286
00:19:06,521 --> 00:19:08,700
The plane's origins date back to the early 70s,

287
00:19:09,040 --> 00:19:15,260
when the Pentagon asked military aircraft makers to submit designs for a fighter jet with reduced radar detectability.

288
00:19:16,620 --> 00:19:22,920
Those with the best design would win a contract to build two demonstration planes, codenamed Have Blue.

289
00:19:23,920 --> 00:19:31,460
In 1975, Dennis Overholzer was a 36-year-old software designer specializing in electromagnetic problems.

290
00:19:32,400 --> 00:19:36,040
In early May, Skunk Works project manager Dick Shearer

291
00:19:36,041 --> 00:19:38,180
asked him to solve an interesting problem.

292
00:19:40,200 --> 00:19:41,920
Dick Shearer then asked me,

293
00:19:42,140 --> 00:19:44,720
how should we shape an airplane to make it invisible?

294
00:19:46,160 --> 00:19:49,920
And I says, well, I says, it's rather straightforward

295
00:19:49,921 --> 00:19:53,420
from a radar cross-section viewpoint.

296
00:19:53,680 --> 00:19:56,100
I says, you just make it out of a lot of flat panels.

297
00:19:56,880 --> 00:19:58,460
You tilt the flat panels over,

298
00:19:58,660 --> 00:20:00,940
and you sweep the edges on the panels.

299
00:20:01,460 --> 00:20:04,860
and you put this together with a bunch of these flat panels.

300
00:20:06,060 --> 00:20:09,400
Well, Dick said, you've got to be kidding me, he said.

301
00:20:09,840 --> 00:20:11,280
Nobody says that.

302
00:20:11,400 --> 00:20:13,040
I said, well, you asked.

303
00:20:13,740 --> 00:20:17,220
Overholzer created new computer software called Echo One.

304
00:20:17,980 --> 00:20:21,040
It calculated how each surface of a plane would scatter radar

305
00:20:21,041 --> 00:20:23,800
or electromagnetic waves that struck its surface.

306
00:20:24,760 --> 00:20:29,380
About a year later, the march toward stealth was advanced by Overholzer's mentor,

307
00:20:29,381 --> 00:20:33,440
a retired former Skunk Works mathematician named Bill Schroeder.

308
00:20:34,260 --> 00:20:37,860
He came across an obscure scientific work written 15 years earlier

309
00:20:37,861 --> 00:20:41,500
by a little-known Russian physicist named Peter Ufimtsev.

310
00:20:42,880 --> 00:20:46,720
Ufimtsev had shown how to predict the directions a geometric configuration

311
00:20:46,721 --> 00:20:49,020
would scatter electromagnetic radiation.

312
00:20:50,740 --> 00:20:54,740
For Skunk Works, it was like finding the Rosetta Stone of stealth technology.

313
00:20:54,741 --> 00:21:01,100
It now had the design principles that would lead to the F-117.

314
00:21:01,101 --> 00:21:06,680
The aircraft was designed 30 years ago by Lockheed Martin in the late 70s.

315
00:21:07,040 --> 00:21:13,100
And what they utilized was a type of stealth technology called a faceted shape design.

316
00:21:14,100 --> 00:21:18,840
And what that does is confuses any kind of radar signal.

317
00:21:18,841 --> 00:21:23,100
and consequently we're able to maintain, for the most part, invisible

318
00:21:23,101 --> 00:21:26,680
against anybody that may be trying to detect our presence out there.

319
00:21:27,560 --> 00:21:33,420
The discovery of Upimcev's work accelerated Skunk Works' plans for a flat panel stealth design.

320
00:21:34,060 --> 00:21:35,860
It looked promising in theory.

321
00:21:36,560 --> 00:21:39,680
The only question now was how to make it fly.

322
00:21:39,681 --> 00:21:48,860
To this day, experimental stealth aircraft are born under a cloak of secrecy.

323
00:21:49,600 --> 00:21:53,540
In October 2002, Boeing unveiled its bird of prey.

324
00:21:54,320 --> 00:21:58,420
The once highly classified project ran from 1992 to 1999,

325
00:21:58,980 --> 00:22:01,540
testing technologies that are now industry standards.

326
00:22:03,700 --> 00:22:07,800
Two more recently revealed top secret programs are a stealth helicopter,

327
00:22:07,801 --> 00:22:14,760
the RAH-66 Comanche, and an unmanned surveillance vehicle called the Dark Star.

328
00:22:15,460 --> 00:22:20,300
But no aircraft's evolution was ever as closely guarded as the F-117s.

329
00:22:20,680 --> 00:22:26,800
In the race for stealth technology in the mid-70s, Lockheed Martin put its money on an ambitious design.

330
00:22:28,120 --> 00:22:32,260
Skunk Works designers created a sketch for a plane made completely of flat sides.

331
00:22:32,261 --> 00:22:38,920
From above, the plane resembled an arrowhead or a diamond that had been beveled in four

332
00:22:38,921 --> 00:22:41,500
primary directions.

333
00:22:41,501 --> 00:22:47,760
My good friend Dick Cantrell was head of aerodynamics and of course, as one might expect, when Dick

334
00:22:47,761 --> 00:22:53,600
first saw some of the stealth designs we were coming up with, specifically our first diamond

335
00:22:53,601 --> 00:23:00,140
shaped object, which he named the Hopeless Diamond, he had some serious doubts about

336
00:23:00,141 --> 00:23:05,640
it and he came to me in kind of a confidence off to the side one day and he said to me,

337
00:23:05,760 --> 00:23:12,223
he said, Dennis, is this really as low a cross section as you're claiming?

338
00:23:12,224 --> 00:23:13,620
And I said, yes,

339
00:23:13,660 --> 00:23:14,360
it is.

340
00:23:14,361 --> 00:23:18,620
Dick thought for a moment and then he says, well, he says, in that case, he says,

341
00:23:18,740 --> 00:23:20,458
we'll teach it to fly.

342
00:23:20,459 --> 00:23:26,080
In 1976, Lockheed took a 10-foot model of their plane to the Air Force's

343
00:23:26,081 --> 00:23:27,266
radar test range.

344
00:23:27,267 --> 00:23:32,360
The results of the tests stunned both DARPA and the Air Force and won the

345
00:23:32,361 --> 00:23:33,399
development contract.

346
00:23:33,400 --> 00:23:37,160
Skunk Works had presented them with an aircraft that had the radar signature

347
00:23:37,161 --> 00:23:41,014
of an insect. The results were dramatic, to say the least.

348
00:23:41,015 --> 00:23:43,140
Two things happened immediately.

349
00:23:43,440 --> 00:23:50,320
The first of which is this fairly unclassified white world program immediately became black and

350
00:23:50,321 --> 00:23:51,520
was classified top secret.

351
00:23:51,860 --> 00:23:53,860
And black means in this context,

352
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,560
not just top secret, but unacknowledged.

353
00:23:57,100 --> 00:23:59,000
It was as black as the Manhattan Project.

354
00:24:00,180 --> 00:24:03,420
The other thing is DARPA and the Air Force and Lockheed

355
00:24:03,421 --> 00:24:06,460
got together and mutually funded the development

356
00:24:06,461 --> 00:24:10,160
of two low-observable technology demonstration aircraft,

357
00:24:10,280 --> 00:24:11,320
the half-blue aircraft.

358
00:24:13,080 --> 00:24:15,780
The challenge now is to build a real airplane

359
00:24:15,781 --> 00:24:18,060
loaded with all kinds of non-stealth things,

360
00:24:18,061 --> 00:24:21,760
like a cockpit, engines, exhausts, and a pilot.

361
00:24:23,440 --> 00:24:26,440
The final half-blue design was like nothing ever built,

362
00:24:26,980 --> 00:24:29,740
perhaps because it was the first ever conceived by electrical

363
00:24:29,741 --> 00:24:31,560
rather than aeronautical engineers.

364
00:24:32,560 --> 00:24:37,940
The plane was 39.8 feet long and weighed around 12,000 pounds.

365
00:24:39,460 --> 00:24:44,060
The leading edges of the wings were swept back to a razor-sharp 70 degrees.

366
00:24:44,061 --> 00:24:52,820
In late November 1977, the engineering team flew Hav Blue to a secret airstrip in the Nevada desert.

367
00:24:53,420 --> 00:24:58,060
It was unloaded at night and placed in a hangar to conceal it from the prying eyes of Soviet satellites.

368
00:24:59,780 --> 00:25:04,860
On December 1, 1977, Hav Blue took to the skies for the first time.

369
00:25:05,580 --> 00:25:08,320
The fact that the plane even flew was an engineering miracle.

370
00:25:08,321 --> 00:25:17,540
The instability of the aircraft in pitch, yaw, and roll, of course, pitch being the longitudinal force of the aircraft, and roll, and yaw, it's side slip.

371
00:25:18,140 --> 00:25:26,160
So the parameters that control those motions of the aircraft were very, very critical in the development of the airplane.

372
00:25:26,520 --> 00:25:34,640
The original half-blue airplane was the first aircraft to ever fly that was unstable in all three axes.

373
00:25:34,641 --> 00:25:40,760
And without fly-by-wire flight control systems, there would not have been a HavBlue airplane.

374
00:25:42,820 --> 00:25:49,020
Fly-by-wire flight control replaces mechanical controls with wires, sensors, computers, and actuators.

375
00:25:49,640 --> 00:25:53,600
In the decades since the flight system helped get HavBlue off the ground,

376
00:25:54,240 --> 00:25:56,920
it's become standard equipment on all stealth aircraft.

377
00:25:56,921 --> 00:26:05,840
And these are the pedal tubes that the aircraft uses to tell it where it's at, how high it is, how fast it's going.

378
00:26:06,280 --> 00:26:10,020
All four of these are hooked up to their own individual computer systems.

379
00:26:10,920 --> 00:26:18,980
Even though the pilot of a fly-by-wire system still uses the standard stick and pedals, the computers assist by making constant adjustments.

380
00:26:18,981 --> 00:26:29,240
On November 16, 1978, the Air Force ordered five full-scale prototypes, or YF-117As.

381
00:26:31,340 --> 00:26:36,500
Skunk Works now had to adapt their design to account for such non-stealth items as a bomb bay,

382
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:41,500
a weapons targeting system, radio antennas, and still keep it stealthy.

383
00:26:41,920 --> 00:26:44,900
The big question was, could it be done?

384
00:26:44,901 --> 00:26:51,880
Stealth technology's prowess in the skies is matched by astronomical budgets.

385
00:26:52,640 --> 00:26:59,880
Current production plans for the F-A-22 Raptor total 279 jets at $133 million each.

386
00:27:00,280 --> 00:27:06,780
In response to such high costs, the Defense Department commissioned the construction of the F-35 Joint Strike Fighter,

387
00:27:07,340 --> 00:27:11,140
a stealth warplane that can be adapted to meet the needs of each branch of the military.

388
00:27:11,141 --> 00:27:18,580
Whether designed for conventional airstrips, aircraft carriers, or even vertical liftoff and landing,

389
00:27:19,400 --> 00:27:25,320
the F-35 brings stealth capability to the field for between $28 and $38 million per plane.

390
00:27:27,880 --> 00:27:33,820
Such flexibility and affordability were unimaginable when history's first stealth aircraft was in development.

391
00:27:33,821 --> 00:27:43,980
The first prototype of the stealth jet, the YF-117A, was completed in the spring of 1981.

392
00:27:44,840 --> 00:27:50,860
It weighed 52,000 pounds and was 65 feet long, much larger than half blue.

393
00:27:52,300 --> 00:27:57,760
The wider shape of the plane met the Air Force's requirement that it carry up to 5,000 pounds of munitions.

394
00:27:58,720 --> 00:28:04,980
To accommodate the internal bomb bay, the prototype's swept wings were much broader than the original half blue.

395
00:28:05,820 --> 00:28:08,200
The F-117 has a similar structure today.

396
00:28:09,480 --> 00:28:12,700
This is commonly referred to as a mile 12.

397
00:28:13,180 --> 00:28:19,360
Anyway, this is where our ordnance hang on, and it's in the down configuration.

398
00:28:19,361 --> 00:28:24,600
It's called a trapeze, and it actually will swing up into the airplane, and then they'll close the doors.

399
00:28:24,601 --> 00:28:29,720
And that reduces our exposure to the radar that are looking at us.

400
00:28:30,200 --> 00:28:35,220
So we come in, the door's open, the bomb comes off, the door's closed very, very quickly.

401
00:28:36,760 --> 00:28:41,820
A desert camouflage scheme disguised the prototype's facets during daytime flight testing.

402
00:28:42,540 --> 00:28:47,720
The Air Force requested that all future planes be painted black for their intended role as night fighters.

403
00:28:48,520 --> 00:28:52,020
The F-117 is designed and has operated at night.

404
00:28:52,021 --> 00:28:55,880
This goes back to the black world origins of the F-117.

405
00:28:56,020 --> 00:28:58,420
It continues to this day. It is a night fighter.

406
00:28:58,920 --> 00:29:01,540
It proves that stealth technology does work.

407
00:29:02,160 --> 00:29:06,320
The F-117 is an incredible aircraft that has far exceeded any expectations.

408
00:29:07,980 --> 00:29:10,420
The Air Force ordered 29 planes.

409
00:29:11,160 --> 00:29:17,520
Eventually, Lockheed would produce 59 F-117s at a cost of $42.6 million per plane.

410
00:29:17,521 --> 00:29:24,320
One of the hardest challenges was the fact that we wanted this airplane to look just like the hopeless diamond.

411
00:29:24,840 --> 00:29:28,280
We wanted perfectly flat angled surfaces to appear to the radar.

412
00:29:29,540 --> 00:29:35,600
But to fly it, we had to take in air, we had to exhaust air, we had to have lights on the wingtips,

413
00:29:35,740 --> 00:29:42,540
we had to have a canopy for the pilot, we had to have an aperture for the infrared system to see through.

414
00:29:42,541 --> 00:29:47,740
So we ended up creating solutions, very innovative solutions to all of those challenges.

415
00:29:51,560 --> 00:29:54,660
Among the most innovative of these was the platypus tail.

416
00:29:55,080 --> 00:30:00,400
What the platypus does is shield the exhaust system of the airplane, which is very flat and very wide.

417
00:30:00,840 --> 00:30:05,700
All of the air that comes out of the engine is hot and washes over the platypus exhaust,

418
00:30:06,040 --> 00:30:09,920
which is basically constructed of high-temperature ceramic brick.

419
00:30:09,921 --> 00:30:14,440
and that brick is almost identical to the space shuttle tiles.

420
00:30:15,860 --> 00:30:21,640
Instead of funneling all of the exhausts in a small location,

421
00:30:21,760 --> 00:30:26,700
what they did is they spread it out and they utilized a special composite material

422
00:30:26,701 --> 00:30:29,380
on the aft end of the tailpipes, if you will,

423
00:30:29,560 --> 00:30:34,680
and what that does is also help to minimize the exhaust temperature.

424
00:30:34,681 --> 00:30:38,040
So hiding not just from the radar lookers,

425
00:30:38,041 --> 00:30:41,120
but also anybody that may be trying to see us

426
00:30:41,121 --> 00:30:42,980
from an infrared standpoint.

427
00:30:44,200 --> 00:30:46,900
To further decrease the prototype's heat signature,

428
00:30:47,620 --> 00:30:49,340
engineers eliminated afterburners,

429
00:30:49,560 --> 00:30:52,120
which raised engine temperature by about 30%.

430
00:30:52,121 --> 00:30:58,220
In October 1979, the Air Force formed

431
00:30:58,221 --> 00:31:01,600
the 4450th Tactical Fighter Wing near Tonopah,

432
00:31:02,060 --> 00:31:03,940
a remote town in the Nevada desert.

433
00:31:03,941 --> 00:31:08,040
The U.S. Air Force gave Colonel Bob Jackson

434
00:31:08,041 --> 00:31:09,900
the task of assembling the elite group

435
00:31:09,901 --> 00:31:12,420
that would make the F-117s operational.

436
00:31:14,460 --> 00:31:16,200
Lieutenant General Dennis R. Larson

437
00:31:16,201 --> 00:31:17,900
was a young captain when he was recruited

438
00:31:17,901 --> 00:31:18,900
for the secret program.

439
00:31:19,820 --> 00:31:21,440
His first interview with Colonel Jackson

440
00:31:21,441 --> 00:31:22,860
was typical of those conducted

441
00:31:22,861 --> 00:31:24,260
with the program's other pilots.

442
00:31:26,900 --> 00:31:28,440
When I walked in the door, he said,

443
00:31:28,520 --> 00:31:29,700
I want to hire you for a job.

444
00:31:30,280 --> 00:31:32,940
All I can tell you is that it's a great job.

445
00:31:33,540 --> 00:31:35,180
I can't tell you where you're going to live,

446
00:31:35,460 --> 00:31:36,780
except for it's going to be in the desert.

447
00:31:37,680 --> 00:31:39,540
I can't tell you whether you're going to be flying.

448
00:31:40,780 --> 00:31:42,120
And that's all I can tell you.

449
00:31:42,540 --> 00:31:43,420
Are you interested?

450
00:31:44,360 --> 00:31:48,080
And I waited about two seconds, I think, or something like that,

451
00:31:48,100 --> 00:31:49,160
and says, I'll take the job.

452
00:31:50,380 --> 00:31:53,340
At Tonopah, pilots were introduced to the black jets

453
00:31:53,341 --> 00:31:55,140
in a scene right out of a Hollywood movie.

454
00:31:55,940 --> 00:31:59,840
Normally we tried to show the pilots the airplane in the dark at night

455
00:31:59,841 --> 00:32:02,280
with the hangar doors just partially opened,

456
00:32:02,281 --> 00:32:06,580
big American flag over the top of the airplane hanging from the top of the hangar,

457
00:32:07,200 --> 00:32:11,140
and your heart just kind of got up into your throat.

458
00:32:12,680 --> 00:32:16,280
For security reasons, pilots were forced to sleep during the day

459
00:32:16,281 --> 00:32:18,820
and fly at night for the first six years.

460
00:32:19,600 --> 00:32:22,940
The people who worked on that fighter when it was still in the black world

461
00:32:22,941 --> 00:32:25,380
referred to this as the vampire convention

462
00:32:25,381 --> 00:32:28,900
because they would have to rush home, get the aircraft in the hangar,

463
00:32:28,901 --> 00:32:30,520
and be back by sunrise.

464
00:32:31,160 --> 00:32:35,540
The F-117A came out of the black on November 10, 1988,

465
00:32:36,280 --> 00:32:39,840
when the Defense Department released this purposely fuzzy photo to the public.

466
00:32:41,080 --> 00:32:46,440
Just 12 days later, the public would get its first look at another top-secret stealth plane,

467
00:32:47,040 --> 00:32:48,000
the B-2 bomber.

468
00:32:48,720 --> 00:32:51,560
By that time, the B-2 had been in development for seven years.

469
00:32:52,720 --> 00:32:58,640
It started in 1981, when Northrop's Advanced Systems Division won an Air Force competition

470
00:32:58,641 --> 00:33:01,340
to design a plane that could fly long distances,

471
00:33:01,900 --> 00:33:05,240
carry a large payload, and invade an enemy's air defense system.

472
00:33:06,860 --> 00:33:08,540
In short, a stealth bomber.

473
00:33:14,380 --> 00:33:17,460
Thanks to a decade of advances in computer technology,

474
00:33:17,960 --> 00:33:21,520
it was now possible to project the results of Ufimtsev's computations

475
00:33:21,521 --> 00:33:23,400
over curved or rounded surfaces.

476
00:33:24,220 --> 00:33:26,880
Instead of flat surfaces like the F-117,

477
00:33:26,881 --> 00:33:29,880
the stealth bomber would have a smooth, curved shape.

478
00:33:30,880 --> 00:33:36,120
The more aerodynamic shape would allow the B-2 to increase its lift and lessen its drag,

479
00:33:36,600 --> 00:33:41,840
enhancing its performance as a long-range aircraft required to carry 40,000 pounds of bombs,

480
00:33:42,280 --> 00:33:43,740
6,000 nautical miles.

481
00:33:45,200 --> 00:33:51,580
The F-117A could employ two weapons against a precision target anywhere in the world at any time.

482
00:33:52,200 --> 00:33:54,560
The B-2 is the next generation of that,

483
00:33:54,561 --> 00:34:00,620
And you've gone from being able to destroy two targets to destroying up to 16 targets at one time.

484
00:34:02,100 --> 00:34:05,280
Northrop based its design on a large flying wing,

485
00:34:05,760 --> 00:34:11,480
an aerodynamic concept pioneered 30 years earlier by the company's legendary founder, Jack Northrop.

486
00:34:13,220 --> 00:34:19,480
In the late 1940s, Northrop had unveiled his innovative giant four-engine bomber, the B-35,

487
00:34:20,140 --> 00:34:22,420
as well as the jet-powered YB-49.

488
00:34:24,560 --> 00:34:30,100
But control systems of that era had trouble making the flying wing a stable bombing platform,

489
00:34:30,460 --> 00:34:32,620
and Washington lost interest in the concept.

490
00:34:38,140 --> 00:34:45,500
Based on the B-2 mission requirements, Northrop engineers determined that the ideal width for the B-2 was 172 feet,

491
00:34:46,000 --> 00:34:49,220
the identical width of Jack Northrop's original flying wing.

492
00:34:49,221 --> 00:34:59,320
One remarkable innovation on the B-2 was the extensive use of carbon fiber epoxy composites.

493
00:35:00,640 --> 00:35:05,960
Lighter than aluminum and stronger than steel, these composites also help absorb radar.

494
00:35:07,120 --> 00:35:12,260
The outboard section of the aircraft included a 65-foot section made entirely of composites.

495
00:35:12,960 --> 00:35:16,560
In fact, the B-2 is made of more pure carbon fiber material than metal.

496
00:35:19,220 --> 00:35:27,400
Both the F-117 and the B-2 reach top speeds of about 650 miles per hour, just shy of Mach 1.

497
00:35:28,680 --> 00:35:31,180
Neither carries guns or air-to-air missiles.

498
00:35:31,740 --> 00:35:35,040
They depend on stealth for survival, not speed or weapons.

499
00:35:36,060 --> 00:35:40,060
Both planes showcase stealth technology's role as a force multiplier.

500
00:35:40,880 --> 00:35:44,620
Force multiplier is the ability to destroy a target,

501
00:35:44,621 --> 00:35:48,840
to inflict the intended damage on an enemy with a smaller amount of force.

502
00:35:49,220 --> 00:35:58,300
In Operation Desert Storm, you have one airplane, the F-117, able to destroy one target, guaranteed with precision accuracy.

503
00:35:58,820 --> 00:36:07,399
When you go on to the next level of technology, you have the B-2, where now with one airplane, you can destroy 16 targets, which really changes the whole nature of

504
00:36:07,400 --> 00:36:08,100
warfare.

505
00:36:08,520 --> 00:36:17,480
We are asked to do a number of different types of missions, one of which is to take down enemy radars, enemy SAM sites, and things like that.

506
00:36:17,481 --> 00:36:20,620
What that does is it enables other aircraft that may not

507
00:36:20,720 --> 00:36:25,120
be as stealthy but may carry a lot more ordnance

508
00:36:25,220 --> 00:36:28,060
fly through that breach in the armor

509
00:36:28,160 --> 00:36:29,900
and then get their job done too.

510
00:36:32,100 --> 00:36:34,900
Stealth's mastery of the skies has become legendary.

511
00:36:35,000 --> 00:36:37,700
But that's not the only place this cutting edge technology

512
00:36:37,800 --> 00:36:39,100
is making a big splash.

513
00:36:39,101 --> 00:36:39,909
flash.

514
00:36:39,910 --> 00:36:52,180
In the mid-1980s, while still top secret itself, the F-117 spawned a surprising descendant,

515
00:36:52,181 --> 00:36:57,820
the Sea Shadow, stealth's first sea craft. Operated by Lockheed Martin for Naval Sea

516
00:36:57,821 --> 00:37:02,220
Systems Command, it has a radar signature no larger than a dinghy's.

517
00:37:02,221 --> 00:37:08,281
One of the most characteristic things about this ship is its faceted hull.

518
00:37:08,282 --> 00:37:09,100
Everything

519
00:37:09,101 --> 00:37:14,240
is at an angle, nothing presents a flat, broadside view anywhere.

520
00:37:15,940 --> 00:37:18,300
Because the sea shadow operates in the water,

521
00:37:18,820 --> 00:37:20,440
it must contend not only with radar,

522
00:37:20,920 --> 00:37:23,960
but also with a second detection system, sonar.

523
00:37:25,940 --> 00:37:28,180
Short for sound navigation ranging,

524
00:37:28,960 --> 00:37:31,120
sonar sends an acoustic pulse through the water.

525
00:37:31,900 --> 00:37:33,680
When the sound waves encounter an obstacle,

526
00:37:33,681 --> 00:37:36,200
they reflect or echo back to the source.

527
00:37:36,960 --> 00:37:40,860
Sonar calculates distances by measuring the time it takes for that echo to return.

528
00:37:42,600 --> 00:37:46,480
A faceted surface proved to be as stealthy in the water as in the sky.

529
00:37:47,180 --> 00:37:50,300
The sea shadow can slip through both radar and sonar.

530
00:37:50,840 --> 00:37:53,920
But in open water, such total success is a liability.

531
00:37:54,800 --> 00:37:59,520
Ocean waves show up on radar, and the sea shadow left an open hole in the water pattern.

532
00:38:00,740 --> 00:38:05,600
Sea Shadow's designers at Lockheed Martin solved this problem, but they're not telling how.

533
00:38:06,200 --> 00:38:15,200
That's no surprise for a vessel born under a cloak of secrecy.

534
00:38:15,201 --> 00:38:21,200
Like the F-117, the Sea Shadow was built under top secret security.

535
00:38:21,201 --> 00:38:26,200
Components were delivered at night and lowered through the retractable roof of this barge.

536
00:38:26,201 --> 00:38:32,200
In 1985, the entire barge transported the vessel to a secret test range for sea trials.

537
00:38:32,201 --> 00:38:36,165
The same barge as Sea Shadow's home today, moored at the U.S.

538
00:38:36,166 --> 00:38:37,920
Naval Station in San Diego.

539
00:38:39,280 --> 00:38:42,940
Sea Shadow is what's commonly referred to as a SWATH hull design.

540
00:38:43,560 --> 00:38:47,640
SWATH stands for Small Water Plane Area Twin Hull Vessel.

541
00:38:49,040 --> 00:38:51,800
The Sea Shadow's hull design is key to its stealth.

542
00:38:53,180 --> 00:38:57,560
Standard V-shaped hulls create a ship's most evident signature, its wake.

543
00:38:58,400 --> 00:39:01,500
WEEKS can be detected not only by radar and sonar operators,

544
00:39:01,880 --> 00:39:06,100
but also by aerial surveillance teams, leaving ships vulnerable to attack.

545
00:39:08,180 --> 00:39:12,660
Sea Shadow's unusual hull is specially designed to avoid creating this telltale signature.

546
00:39:13,320 --> 00:39:19,220
At the end of each strut is a lower hull section with the length of the ship and about 10 feet in diameter.

547
00:39:20,040 --> 00:39:24,780
The only part that actually cuts the surface of the water are the two struts that go to the lower hulls,

548
00:39:24,781 --> 00:39:28,120
and they're very long and narrow and pointed on the ends,

549
00:39:28,360 --> 00:39:31,200
so they create little to no wake as we go through the water.

550
00:39:34,020 --> 00:39:37,200
The near-constant vibration of engines and generators,

551
00:39:37,660 --> 00:39:39,240
a dead giveaway to sonar detection,

552
00:39:39,780 --> 00:39:42,260
presents another challenge to stealth technology at sea.

553
00:39:43,360 --> 00:39:46,160
We have vibration eliminators in all piping systems.

554
00:39:46,380 --> 00:39:49,160
Most have two angles to them.

555
00:39:49,161 --> 00:39:53,280
You have a vertical vibration eliminator and a horizontal eliminator.

556
00:39:53,281 --> 00:39:58,780
We'll go around the corner and we will see another type of system that eliminates the

557
00:39:58,781 --> 00:40:01,220
vibration from the equipment itself.

558
00:40:01,221 --> 00:40:05,820
This very large engine is mounted on four vibration eliminators.

559
00:40:05,821 --> 00:40:10,900
Those are in the foundation of the unit and they prevent the vibration and the noise from

560
00:40:10,901 --> 00:40:15,660
this large diesel engine from transmitting to the hull and thus into the water.

561
00:40:15,661 --> 00:40:19,520
Those four mounts are located at each corner.

562
00:40:19,521 --> 00:40:21,900
You can see that they're a thick substance.

563
00:40:21,901 --> 00:40:24,560
They're in elastomeric rubber.

564
00:40:24,561 --> 00:40:27,440
The bolts go through those to prevent the machinery

565
00:40:27,441 --> 00:40:31,180
from tilting, but yet the machinery is totally isolated

566
00:40:31,181 --> 00:40:35,300
from the steel hull of the ship through that cushion.

567
00:40:35,301 --> 00:40:38,100
Inside and out, every element of the sea shadow

568
00:40:38,101 --> 00:40:40,380
contributes to maximum stealth.

569
00:40:40,381 --> 00:40:42,960
Topside hatches have sawtoothed edges

570
00:40:42,961 --> 00:40:45,480
and retractable cleats for mooring lines.

571
00:40:45,481 --> 00:40:49,960
If we were in a situation where we might want to clean

572
00:40:49,961 --> 00:40:53,580
the deck and reduce our signature.

573
00:40:53,581 --> 00:40:55,920
We can dismount all the antennas from topside

574
00:40:55,921 --> 00:40:58,400
and stow them below.

575
00:40:58,401 --> 00:41:00,020
But normally when we're at sea, we

576
00:41:00,021 --> 00:41:01,380
don't want to operate that way.

577
00:41:01,381 --> 00:41:03,620
We want to be seen by other vessels,

578
00:41:03,621 --> 00:41:05,160
and see other vessels.

579
00:41:05,161 --> 00:41:09,140
And we need these sensors and antennas to do that.

580
00:41:09,141 --> 00:41:10,940
Design elements from the sea shadow

581
00:41:10,941 --> 00:41:14,580
are now being built into a new generation of naval vessels.

582
00:41:14,581 --> 00:41:17,320
These warships with reduced radar signatures

583
00:41:17,321 --> 00:41:19,680
signify that stealth is coming of age at sea.

584
00:41:20,220 --> 00:41:22,880
Some of the technology that I know is carried over

585
00:41:22,881 --> 00:41:25,180
is its faceted hull,

586
00:41:25,680 --> 00:41:28,880
and you'll see that now in DDG-51,

587
00:41:29,080 --> 00:41:30,680
Arleigh Burke-class destroyers,

588
00:41:30,880 --> 00:41:32,180
the LPD-17,

589
00:41:32,980 --> 00:41:34,840
very clean superstructures,

590
00:41:35,560 --> 00:41:38,020
some gentle angles to the ships.

591
00:41:38,680 --> 00:41:41,520
A lot of that is derived,

592
00:41:41,740 --> 00:41:43,000
has been derived from the testing

593
00:41:43,001 --> 00:41:44,100
that was done on Sea Shadow.

594
00:41:44,101 --> 00:41:50,500
Time will tell if stealth's impact on naval warfare will be as dramatic as its effect in the skies.

595
00:41:51,780 --> 00:41:57,560
On the sea and in the air, stealth tech may progress beyond what's now imaginable.

596
00:41:59,140 --> 00:42:03,460
The continual research and development of stealth technology is absolutely essential.

597
00:42:03,740 --> 00:42:07,960
We've almost developed a level of complacency because we've been so good for so long.

598
00:42:08,180 --> 00:42:12,700
We've had the best aircraft in the world, but you have to continue to work to have that.

599
00:42:12,701 --> 00:42:19,200
When we go to war, we don't want to go to war with somebody who has a near capability to us or even an equal capability.

600
00:42:19,780 --> 00:42:23,360
We want to have overwhelming power. We want to have air dominance.

601
00:42:23,880 --> 00:42:25,680
And stealth technology is essential to that.

602
00:42:26,260 --> 00:42:34,960
I think it's important that research and development, places like the Skunk Works, continue to reach out, continue to take risk, and sometimes fail.

603
00:42:35,520 --> 00:42:41,380
And I think the real issue today is we have become such a risk-averse society.

604
00:42:41,381 --> 00:42:44,480
failure is just not accepted,

605
00:42:45,260 --> 00:42:47,780
that by virtue of not taking those risks,

606
00:42:48,140 --> 00:42:50,440
we are not going to discover the next technologies

607
00:42:50,441 --> 00:42:51,580
that need to be discovered

608
00:42:51,581 --> 00:42:55,520
to truly make our force affordable and invincible.

609
00:42:56,720 --> 00:42:59,700
And don't forget that we didn't see the F-117

610
00:42:59,701 --> 00:43:01,760
until a decade after it first flew.

611
00:43:02,780 --> 00:43:05,160
Who knows what amazing stealth technology

612
00:43:05,161 --> 00:43:06,900
may already be out there.

